  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather will prevail into the weekend \nwith no significant weather hazards anticipated.\n\nAloft, a closed low will traverse portions of Ontario and the \nHudson Bay and into Quebec through Friday, with long wave \ntroughing across much of the northeastern US. Mid-level ridging \nwill briefly build into the region for Saturday. At the surface,\na weak cold front will pass through the region today before high\npressure returns for Friday and Saturday.\n\nAfter the cooler temperatures the past couple of days, a brief \nwarmup is anticipated, with highs today in the mid 70s. The \ncold frontal passage today will usher in drier air and nudge \ntemperatures down a bit for tonight and into the first part of \nthe weekend. Near- normal temperatures are expected tonight \nthrough Saturday, with lows in the mid 40s to low 50s and highs \nin the low to mid 70s for most locations. The cold frontal \npassage itself is expected to be dry, so tranquil conditions are\nexpected through Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The remnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias \nwill approach the area late this weekend bringing rain back to \nthe region Sunday into Sunday night.\n\nThe primary forecast concern for the latter part of this \nweekend and perhaps into early next week will be the remnants of\nwhat is currently Hurricane Isaias in the Gulf. It is expected \nto intensify further before making landfall somewhere along the\nnorthern Gulf coast Friday night as it begins interacting with \nan upper trough. From there, it is expected to undergo \nextratropical transition and continue northward into portions of\nthe Ohio Valley through Saturday night, and then northeastward \nin the direction of our area on Sunday. \n\nTiming discrepancies remain amongst model guidance this \nafternoon, but there is strong agreement that the remnants of \nIsaias will bring some impacts to the region Sunday and perhaps \ninto early Monday. At this point in time, it appears that \nwidespread rain will be the primary impact. It should be noted \nthat PWATs are modeled to be in the 1.5-2 inch range, so some \nperiods of heavy rainfall will be possible. WPC has placed most \nof the region in a Marginal Risk (1 of 4) Excessive Rainfall \nOutlook for Sunday.\n\nIt is too early to specify exact rainfall amounts, but \nconfidence is higher than normal that a large portion of the \narea could end up with 1 to 2 inches of rain, with higher \namounts certainly not out of the question. We will continue to \nmonitor trends over the coming days.",
